lib/law/accountable-attention-and-publication.json:
{"kind":"koios.publication-law","id":"accountable-attention-and-publication","purpose":"Compete for useful attention without allowing demand, compression, generation, advertising, or delivery to manufacture warrant.","invariants":["Demand may create an assignment but never evidence.","A source statement remains attributable to its source until an authorized editorial act adopts a narrower newsroom claim.","No public surface may create a factual commitment not licensed by the article, its cited evidence, its uncertainty, or a declared editorial stance.","A manuscript is not publishable unless deterministic gates clear and the latest exact-manuscript independent editorial judgment accepts it.","Corrections, clarifications, updates, and retractions must propagate to every dependent headline, article, feed, card, sitemap, metadata record, and syndication surface that Koios controls.","Audience demand, attention, advertising, revenue, provider cost, and editorial warrant are recorded as distinct facts and never substituted for one another.","Newsroom learning may propose a replay, route review, observation period, or standards review from durable outcomes; it may not itself publish, execute the replay, alter a route, or amend law.","LLMs may produce prose, images, or bounded choices; deterministic code owns serialization, digests, admission, ledgers, and publication transitions.","A provider is selected by the cheapest route that satisfies the declared quality and evidence requirements; zero marginal price never waives those requirements.","No deployment target becomes authoritative without parity evidence, an accepted resource grant, a rollback route, and an explicit cutover act.","A public privacy surface must describe the requests the exact publication artifact actually emits; suspended advertising or attention may not be described as active, and active third-party carriers may not be described as absent.","Advertising units, placements, provider requests, seller declarations, performance receipts, and revenue enter Koios only under provider-authored or provider-accepted evidence; Koios never invents ads.txt authority."],"derivedFrom":["dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/ietf-conformant-clickbait.md","dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/accountable-attention.md","dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/agentic-newsroom.md","dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/claims-to-prose.md","dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/news-topology.md"],"enforcedBy":["src/researching/writing/editing/standards.mts","src/researching/writing/editing/liss-editorial.mts","src/management/newsroom-learning.mts","src/researching/writing/editing/publishing/managing/circulating/index.mts","src/administrating/deploying/static-shadow.mts","src/administrating/deploying/static-profile-parity.mts"]}

lib/law/reported-analysis-genre-contract.json:
{"kind":"koios.genre-contract","id":"reported-analysis/v1","promise":"A timely report organized around one witnessed event, with Koios's systems interpretation visibly subordinate to and licensed by the reporting.","evidenceBudget":{"targetWords":{"minimum":800,"maximum":1100},"minimumDistinctAssertions":15,"minimumPrimaryAssertions":15,"maximumWordsPerAssertion":55,"law":"The requested length is licensed only when the admitted packet carries enough distinct source-bound assertions to sustain it. When the budget does not clear, acquire reporting or commission a narrower genre before writer inference."},"writerPromptLaws":["Write reported analysis, not a thematic essay: the event and the people, institutions, places, dates, quantities, documents, and decisions in the sources remain the article's spine.","Before drafting, silently inventory the packet's supported claims and distinct new details; every paragraph and every section must advance at least one of them rather than restating the frame.","Treat each source as evidence only for its own actors, place, time, scope, attribution, and modality; never use thematic similarity as a bridge between different events.","Koios's systems interpretation may explain a pattern made visible by supported facts, but it may not supply an unreported causal mechanism, motive, consequence, category, or analogy.","Use straight attribution when the source is an allegation, estimate, forecast, interpretation, institutional statement, or contested claim; do not launder it into Koios's voice.","Model output is never evidence. Prefer original and named sources, preserve source motives and uncertainty, and treat consequential claims as publishable only to the extent they are corroborated by the admitted packet.","When the story criticizes a person or institution, alleges wrongdoing, or reports a dispute, represent the subject's response or state only what the admitted reporting establishes about a documented opportunity to respond; otherwise return REPORTING_NEEDED.","Avoid needless harm, sensationalism, stereotypes, unattributed opinion, and commercial influence; keep reported analysis visibly distinct from advocacy and advertising.","A headline may intensify relevance but not certainty; it names the subject before withholding an answer and preserves material attribution, uncertainty, scope, and correction state.","The lede tells the reader what happened, where, to whom, and why it matters now; do not open with a universal thesis, scene-setting abstraction, or generic collapse language.","Do not create a section unless the packet supplies a distinct factual development for it; if fewer than three sections are supportable, refuse with REPORTING_NEEDED instead of padding.","The ending must close on the most consequential supported unresolved fact, decision, deadline, conflict, or question facing the named actors; never substitute an abstract meditation for reporting.","Give readers enough factual payoff in the headline, description, summary, and lede to make an informed click or no-click decision; withhold an answer when useful, never the subject."],"gates":["event spine","paragraph-level source warrant","attribution and modality preservation","headline-body congruity","non-repetition","correction readiness","exact-manuscript independent editorial acceptance"],"derivedFrom":["dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/journalism.md","dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/agentic-newsroom.md","dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/circulation-surface.md","dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/ietf-conformant-clickbait.md","dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/accountable-attention.md","dev/content/notes/2026/06/01/clickbait.md"]}

INDEPENDENT EDITOR REPORT:

## Editorial Assessment â€” reporting-source-1-for-odyssey-becomes-christopher-nolan-biggest

### Pipeline Diagnosis

Sources are adequate: four trade outlets (Times of India, Variety, LA Times, Deadline) all published 2026-07-19 cover the $264.1 million global debut, the Dark Knight Rises comparison, the IMAX production history, and the competitive box office context. The assignment is valid. The failure layer is the writer model, which produced a draft with a hard gate error, two factual misrepresentations, systematic citation link text corruption, near-duplicate prose passages, and garbled carrier surfaces.

---

### Deterministic Gate Failures

**ERROR â€” body-too-short (578 words; gate requires 600).**
The draft is 22 words below the publishable floor. This is a hard failure that independently blocks publication regardless of other defects.

**WARNING Ã— 4 â€” near-duplicate-proximity.**
The copy-editor gate identifies four sentence pairs that repeat multiple short phrases within the same paragraph. Three pairs are concentrated in the "A Calibration of Scale and Craft" section, where the budget sentence, the IMAX-anticipation sentence, and the IMAX-shot-entirely sentence cycle through the same production and format vocabulary without advancing distinct claims. A fourth pair is in "Systemic Integration in Exhibition," where the summer trailing sentence and the dominant-position sentence overlap.

**WARNING â€” demand-lens-missing-h2.**
No level-two heading naturally expresses the central event's significance (Nolan's biggest global debut). "A Calibration of Scale and Craft" and "Systemic Integration in Exhibition" are abstract; neither signals what a reader seeking the debut story would expect to find. This is a warning, not a hard gate failure, but it compounds the near-duplicate weakness.

---

### Material Factual Audit

**Lede and debut figure: supported.**
The $264.1 million global debut is confirmed across ToI ($124.5M domestic + $139.6M international), Variety (same figures), LA Times (studio estimates), and Deadline ($264.1M worldwide, ahead of Dark Knight Rises at $249M WW). The framing as Nolan's biggest global opening is warranted by all four sources.

**Budget ($200Mâ€“$250M): supported.**
LA Times and ToI both report this range; the manuscript cites both sources for this claim. âœ“

**Shot entirely with IMAX cameras: supported.**
LA Times and ToI both confirm this as the first feature shot entirely on Imax cameras. âœ“

**Practical effects (animatronics, Cyclops, Viking boat): supported.**
LA Times source describes puppetry, animatronics, robotics for Cyclops scenes and a real Viking boat the actors sailed. The manuscript describes this as "a deliberate departure from contemporary digital-heavy standards," which imports an editorial gloss not present in the source text; the source characterizes it as adding "mystique," not as a departure statement. Borderline but not a clear misrepresentation.

**95% critical approval: supported (attribution slightly loose).**
ToI says "95% fresh on Rotten Tomatoes." The manuscript says "95% critical approval rating" without naming Rotten Tomatoes. The omission weakens attribution but is not a fabrication.

**IMAX 4% claim: FACTUAL MISREPRESENTATION.**
The manuscript states: "IMAX, in particular, played a central role in the film's financial performance, with the format accounting for 4% of the total box office haul."

The LA Times source (evidence card `bd7c6f1462b3ca8f4d623b38b4e627013f9405d16a050668ba9fa10022d4c550:16`) specifies: "Enhanced formats made up 53% of the domestic weekend's total, with both film and digital Imax revenue comprising 23.8%. Imax 70mm comprised about 4% of that total, with Imax digital making up the rest."

"That total" refers to the enhanced-format portion (53% of domestic gross), not the overall box office. Imax 70mm was approximately 4% of 53% â‰ˆ 2% of domestic gross â€” a sub-format share within a sub-total. The manuscript treats this sub-category share as if it were all of IMAX's contribution to the overall haul. By contrast, the ToI source reports IMAX grossed $51.8 million globally â€” approximately 19.6% of the $264.1 million total â€” which sharply contradicts a "4% of total" reading. The manuscript's sentence materially understates and mislabels IMAX's contribution.

**Summer box office comparison: FACTUAL MISREPRESENTATION.**
The manuscript states: "summer-to-date box office revenue is trailing 2019 levels by roughly 7% to 9%."

The LA Times source (evidence card `bd7c6f1462b3ca8f4d623b38b4e627013f9405d16a050668ba9fa10022d4c550:19`) reports two distinct numbers: summer-to-date is "about 7% higher than the same time period last year" AND "still lags pre-pandemic summer 2019 revenue by about 9%." These are different comparators â€” one is a year-over-year gain, the other is the deficit against a pre-pandemic baseline. The manuscript collapses them into a single "7% to 9%" range that describes only trailing 2019, falsely converting the year-over-year improvement into a second measure of the 2019 deficit.

---

### Citation Link Text Audit

Writing law requires exact character-for-character reproduction of source titles in link text. Every inline citation in this manuscript violates that requirement.

**Times of India title (appears 5 times in body):**
- Source title: `'The Odyssey' storms box office with a USD 264.1 million global debut; dethrones 'The Dark Knight Rises' as Christopher Nolan's highest film opening`
- Manuscript link text: `The Odyssey storms box office with a USD 264.1 million global debut, dethrones The Dark Knight Rises as Christopher Nolan's highest film opening`
- Alterations: apostrophes around both film names removed; semicolon after "global debut" replaced with comma.

**Deadline title (appears once in body):**
- Source title: `'The Odyssey' Conquers $264M+ Around The World, Best Ever For Christopher Nolan Ahead Of 'Dark Knight Rises' â€“ Global B.O. Sunday Update`
- Manuscript link text: `The Odyssey conquers $264M around the world, best ever for Christopher Nolan ahead of Dark Knight Rises â€“ Global B.O. Sunday Update`
- Alterations: apostrophes removed; "Conquers" â†’ "conquers"; "$264M+" â†’ "$264M" (drops the "+" indicating the figure exceeded that threshold); title-case "The World," "Best," "Ever," "For," "Ahead," "Of" â†’ sentence case throughout.

**LA Times title (appears 6 times in body):**
- Source title: `'The Odyssey' is Christopher Nolan's biggest global box office opening`
- Manuscript link text: `Christopher Nolan's 'The Odyssey' box office`
- Alteration: entire title replaced with a shortened informal paraphrase.

These are not typographic slips â€” every citation is systematically altered. The prevalence across all three sources and all 12 body citation instances points to a systemic writer-model behavior, not an isolated error.

---

### Carrier Surface Defects

The `description` field is truncated mid-sentence: "The latest feature film from Christopher Nolan, The Odyssey, has set a new benchmark for the director's career by securing a $264." The sentence ends at the dollar amount without the number.

The `summary` field contains: "...securing a $264. O." â€” a corrupted token sequence â€” followed by a sentence not found in the body, ending with what appears to be a floating source title fragment ("Christopher Nolan's 'The Odyssey' box office").

The `tweet` field reproduces the headline verbatim rather than functioning as a share sentence.

These surface defects are consistent with writer-model output truncation and would be regenerated by `repair_editorial_surfaces` â€” but only after the body's factual errors are corrected, since surfaces are projected from admitted body content.

---

### Fairness Assessment

The manuscript briefly reports the casting controversy (Elon Musk's comments on Lupita Nyong'o's casting) and characterizes it as having "little to no effect" on the film's performance â€” a framing directly attributed to the ToI source. No allegation of wrongdoing is lodged against any individual; the controversy note is a contextual observation with source backing. No fairness obligation is triggered.

---

### Replay Scope

A writer replay is required. The citation link text corruption is distributed across all three sources and 12 body locations; exact bounded repair across 10+ unique spans is operationally infeasible without unacceptable risk of partial repair collisions, especially given the simultaneous need to expand word count and rewrite near-duplicate pairs. The two factual corrections are individually bounded, but performing them before a writer replay that would rewrite the same sections wastes editorial work. The writer replay should produce: a body exceeding 600 words with no duplicate phrase repetition across adjacent sentences; correct citation link texts reproducing source titles character-for-character; and factually accurate representations of the IMAX format revenue breakdown and the year-over-year versus 2019 summer box office comparisons.


